## Introduction
Advances in electronics and electric vehicles call for higher-energydensity energy storage and conversion devices [1–3]. Compared to the current commercial lithium-ion batteries, lithium-sulfur (Li-S) batteries  are  considered  as  a  potential  alternative  to  avoid  the limitation of cathode capacity and anode mismatch in lithium-ion batteries due to the high theoretical capacity $(1,675\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}}^{\cdot}$ ) and energy density $(2,600\ \mathrm{Wh{\cdot}k g^{-1}})$ ) [4, 5]. Moreover, the low cost and non-toxic  nature  of  sulfur  offer  economic  benefits  and environmental  friendliness.  Unfortunately,  several  technical challenges still hamper the advancement of Li-S batteries, such as the inherent poor electronic conductivity of sulfur $(5\times10^{-30}\mathrm{S}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at room temperature), the undesirable large volume variation of the  sulfur  cathode $(80\%)$ ,  as  well  as  the  dissolution  of  lithium polysulfides (LiPSs) with a notorious shuttle effect [6–8]. Together, these  obstacles  bring  about  fast  capacity  decay  and  poor  rate performance of Li-S batteries.
In response to the aforementioned challenges, a typical strategy is  encapsulating  sulfur  into  porous  carbonaceous  materials  for improving  the  cathode  conductivity  and  relieving  the  LiPSs migration  owing  to  the  high  conductivity  and  abundant  pore structure [9, 10]. However, the intrinsically non-polar carbonaceous  materials  can  only  bind  LiPSs  within  a  few  cycles through a weak van der Waals force, and then the battery capacity declines rapidly [11, 12]. Given this, polar transition metal oxides and  sulfides  have  drawn  increasing  attention  as  potential  sulfur hosts  owing  to  the  strong  LiPSs  anchoring  ability  [13, 14]. Unfortunately,  most  of  these  materials  still  do  not  meet  the practical  requirements  of  Li-S  batteries,  especially  in  high  sulfur contents or poor electrolyte/sulfur (E/S) ratios due to the difficulty in anchoring excess LiPSs with oxides and sulfides [15]. Recently, introducing electrocatalysts to facilitate sulfur species conversion has been considered as a valid strategy to improve the rate and cycle performance of Li-S batteries given the multi-electron redox reactions that occur during the cycle [16–18].
Layered  transition  metal  selenium  compounds  possess attractive  physical/chemical  properties  such  as  high  specific surface area, impressive catalytic effect for sulfur conversion, and good compatibility with sulfur and lithium sulfide, showing great potential  as  sulfur  hosts  [19, 20].  Recent  studies  found  that selenium  and  sulfur  can  form  selenium-sulfur  solid  solution $(\mathrm{Se}_{x}\mathrm{S}_{y})$ bathing suit, thus improving the electrical conductivity and reactivity of sulfur [21]. Bismuth selenide $\left(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3}\right)$ , with a desirable interfacial charge feature and narrow bandgap of $0.3\:\mathrm{eV}$ , has been widely  studied  in  the  field  of  thermoelectric  and  optoelectronic devices  [22].  In  particular,  its  two-dimensional  layered  structure and desirable interfacial charge feature make it widely studied in the electrode materials of both lithium-ion batteries and sodiumion  batteries  [23].  These  advantages  make ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3}$ promising  in catalytic  and  stable  sulfur  electrochemistry.  However,  the catalytical  active  sites  in  two-dimensional  materials  are  mainly concentrated at the edges, and the basal planes are almost inert to catalyze  redox  reactions  [24–26].  In  this  regard,  the  strategy  of anionic vacancy design has been proposed to further enhance the electrochemical  performance  of  two-dimensional materials  [23, 27, 28]. The rationally introduced anionic vacancies can optimize the band structure, charge distribution, and crystal structure of the target materials and thus improve their intrinsic properties such as electronic/ionic  transportation  properties,  adsorption  ability, catalytic activity, and structural durability. Therefore, introducing anionic  vacancy  may  also  be  an  effective  way  to  enhance  the electrochemical performance of ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3}$ as a sulfur host. Although the  enhanced  mechanism  of  anionic  vacancies  has  been  widely explored [17, 28], the influence of anionic vacancies on the sulfur redox  reaction  process  in  Li-S  batteries  has  not  been  well understood, especially for Se vacancy.
Considering  this,  a  series  of  heterogeneous  composites $(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC})$ with adjustable Se vacancy contents are developed by  selenizing  Bi-based  metal-organic  framework  (Bi-MOFs) precursor and subsequent thermal shock, and employed as sulfur host. $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}$ with  different  numbers  of  selenium  vacancies  was also  prepared  quantitatively  by  controlling  the  annealing temperature and time. The MOFs-derived porous nitrogen-doped carbon can inhibit volume expansion of sulfur electrodes, localize sulfur species, and offer abundant active interfaces for electronic transmission.  Meanwhile,  defect  engineering  and  structure features not only endow $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}$ with enhanced conductivity and sulfophilic property but also promote the conversion reaction of sulfur species, which allows for effective shuttle suppression and fast  sulfur  redox  kinetics.  In  addition, $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}$ with  the  suitable amount  of  selenium  vacancies  achieves  the  best  electrochemical performance  due  to  the  advantages  of  its  structure  and composition.  These  results  confirm  the  intrinsic  correlation between defects and catalysis, which are revealed by computational and experimental studies. As a result, $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}$ host exhibits admirable stability with a fairly lower capacity decay rate of approximately $0.0278\%$ per cycle over 1,000 cycles at a $3\mathrm{C}$ rate, and still demonstrates a high discharge capacity of $455\mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg^{-1}}}$ at $^\mathrm{~1~C~}$ even  at  a  high  sulfur  content  of $6.2\ \mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .  This  work uncovers  the  enhanced  mechanism  of $\mathsf{S e}$ vacancy  and  may enlighten  the  development  of  anionic  vacancies  toward  highperformance Li-S batteries.
## 2    Results and discussion
Figure  1(a) illustrates  the  synthesis  route  of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ heterogeneous  composites.  In  particular,  Bi-MOFs  precursor  is synthesized by simple hydrothermal method, and then selenized and pyrolyzed to obtain two-dimensional ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3}$ nanosheets and nitrogen-doped  composites.  Finally,  defect-rich $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ is produced  by  the  following  thermal  shock  process.  The  field emission scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images display the uniform  morphology  and  structure  of  Bi-MOFs  (Fig. 1(b)), ${\mathrm{Bi}_{2}S e_{3}}/{\mathrm{NC}}$ (Fig. 1(c)),  and $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ (Fig. 1(d)),  respectively.
To reveal the inherent physical and chemical advantages of the defect-rich $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC},$ a  series  of  characterization  methods  are used  to  study  and  compare  the  related  properties  of  the  two samples. X-ray diffraction (XRD) is employed to study the effect of  defect  engineering  on  the  crystallization  and  composition properties of the samples. As shown in Fig. 2(a), both $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3}/\mathrm{NC}$ and $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ demonstrate  a  series  of  sharp  diffraction  peaks matching  the ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3}$ phase  (PDF#89-2008)  in  good  condition, suggesting their crystal structures are consistent with the HRTEM results.  Remarkably,  magnified  XRD  patterns  of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3}/\mathrm{NC}$ and $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ also display the left-shifted peak and increased peak value  of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ compared  with $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3}/\mathrm{NC}$ (Fig. 2(b)), especially  in  the  small-angle  range,  which  is  explained  as  the influence of selenium vacancies. The chemical states of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3}/\mathrm{NC}$ and $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ are  ascertained  by  X-ray  photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). Bi, Se, C, N, and O five elements are present in $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ composites revealed by the XPS survey spectrum in Fig. S2(a) in the ESM. As shown in Fig. 2(d), two typical peaks can be observed in the high-resolution Bi 4f XPS spectra of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3}/\mathrm{NC}$ at  158  and $163.3~\mathrm{{\eV}_{:}}$ ,  corresponding  to  Bi $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ and $\scriptstyle4\mathrm{f}_{5/2},$ respectively. In comparison, $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ shows a pair of peaks at 157.7 and $163~\mathrm{eV};$ , displaying the slightly negative shift of $0.3~\mathrm{eV}_{:}$ , which is attributed to the increased electron cloud density of the Bi atom  due  to  the  reduced  Se  coordination  [29].  Moreover,  the Se 3d high-resolution spectra for $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3}/\mathrm{NC}$ and $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ are demonstrated  in Fig. 2(e).  The  two  typical  peaks  at  54.3  and $53.4~\mathrm{{\eV}}$ are  assigned  to  the $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ levels  of ${\mathsf{S e}}^{2-}$ , respectively. Meanwhile, the typical peak at $55.9\mathrm{eV}$ is thought to form the Bi–Se bond. In contrast, a new $\mathrm{Se^{0}}$ peak at $56.8~\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ confirms  the  introduction  and  increase  of  selenium vacancies with the thermal shock process [30]. Additionally, N 1s spectrum  attested  to  the  existence  of  pyridinic-N  at $398.5\ \mathrm{eV};$ nitrile-N at $400~\mathrm{eV}_{:}$ , pyrrolic- $.\mathrm{N}$ at $401.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ , graphitic-N at 402.9 $\mathrm{eV}$ , and oxidized-N at $404.2\:\mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. S2(b) in the ESM). Besides, C 1s spectra are divided into three peaks, namely, C–C $(284.8~\mathrm{eV})$ , C–N (287 eV), and $scriptstyle\mathrm{O=C-N}$ $(288.5~\mathrm{eV})$ (Fig. S2(c) in the ESM). These  results  further  confirm  that $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ is  successfully synthesized.  The  electron  paramagnetic  resonance  (EPR)  test  is employed  to  further  confirm  the  defect  characteristic  of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ composites. The EPR spectrum displays a sharp EPR signal  at $g$ value  2.009  for $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ compared  with  the  flat spectrum of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3}/\mathrm{NC}$ in response to the presence of abundant selenium  vacancies  generated  during  the  preparation  process, which is shown in Fig. 2(c). Moreover, Figs. 2(f) and $2(\mathrm{g)}$ depict the density of state (DOS) mode of ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}$ simulated by  first-principles  calculations  according  to  the  electron distributions  of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}$ and ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3}$ .  The  bandgap  of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}$ is significantly  narrowed  after  the  introduction  of  selenium vacancies. Distinctly, there exist prominent impurity states at the Fermi  level  of ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3-x},$ indicating  that  electrons  might  cross  the Fermi  level  and  the  electronic  conductivity  is  improved accordingly.  All  the  above  results  determine  that  the  electronic structure  of ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3}$ changes  significantly  after  the  successful elimination of partial Se, and the electron distribution around the Bi atom is optimized, thus enhancing the adsorption and catalytic effect for the promoted performance of Li-S batteries.
Given the above results, the $\ensuremath{a}{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{Bi}_{2}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{e}}_{3-x}/\ensuremath{\mathrm{NC}}$ $a=0;$ , 20, 40, 60, 80, and 100) with different selenium vacancies levels are prepared by changing the thermal shock time to study the effect of selenium vacancies on the crystallization and composition of the product. Table S1 in the ESM summarizes the ratio of Se atoms to Bi atoms in samples with different defect levels. The atomic ratio continues to decrease from $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3}/\mathrm{NC}$ to $100{\cdot}\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC},$ which confirms the increase of selenium vacancies upon defect introduction. As depicted  in Fig. S3  in  the  ESM,  the  XRD  results  show  that  the XRD  peak  of $0{\mathrm{-Bi}}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3}/\mathrm{NC}$ matches  the ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3}$ phase  (PDF#89- 2008)  completely.  When  the  thermal  shock  time  increases  to $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ ,  the  corresponding  peaks  are  shifted  to  the  left  in  turn, indicating  the  presence  of  selenium  vacancies  and  its  increased content  in ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3-x}/{\mathrm{NC}}.$ As  the  thermal  shock  continues  to increase, the crystal integrity of ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3}$ is constantly damaged, while new  phase  Bi  metal  appears  until  the  thermal  shock  time  of $100~\mathrm{{min}}$ , indicating that excessive thermal shock resulted in the reduction  of  a  large  amount  of ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3}$ .  In  addition,  the conductivities of $\ensuremath{a}{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{Bi}_{2}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{e}}_{3-x}/\ensuremath{\mathrm{NC}}$ with different selenium vacancies levels are tested, showing that the conductivity of $60{-}\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ is the highest in a series of samples, which is more favorable for electrons/ions  transport  and  sulfur  species  redox  reactions (Fig. $2\mathrm{(h)},$ .  Therefore, $60{-}\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ is  employed  as  the  best defect product for further study, labeled as ${\mathrm{Bi}_{2}S e_{3-x}}/{\mathrm{NC}}.$
To investigate the practical effect of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ as a sulfur host, the composite cathode electrodes $(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ and $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S})$ were  prepared  by  melt  impregnation  process.  As  shown  in Fig. 2(i),  the  thermal  gravimetric  analysis  (TGA)  test  reveals  the elemental sulfur loading in contrast sample and $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ is 74.75 and $77.84\mathrm{~wt.\%}$ , respectively. The high sulfur loading results from  the  porous  structure  of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ that  provides  more sufficient  space  for  sulfur.  Moreover, $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ has  a  higher sulfur  release  temperature,  indicating  that  selenium  vacancies enhance  the  adsorption  of  sulfur  [31].  In  addition,  energy dispersive  X-ray  (EDX)  elemental  mapping  of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ composite manifests that the element sulfur is evenly distributed in $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC},$ which  further  indicates  that $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ was synthesized  by  the  melt  impregnation  process  (Fig. S4  in the ESM).
Button batteries (CR2032) with different hosts were assembled in an argon glove box and evaluated for performance. Figure 3(a) displays  typical  cyclic  voltammetry  (CV)  curves  for  the $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ electrode at $0.1\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ rate with two clear oxidation peaks interpreted as the conversion of short-chain sulfur species to long-chain sulfur species, and the two reduction peaks represented the conversion of long-chain sulfur species to short-chain sulfur species.  Apparently,  the  CV  curves  of  the  first  three  cycles  of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ almost  coincide,  which  implies  the  stable  and reversible operation of the sulfur cathode. As shown in Fig. S5 in the ESM, compared with the CV curves of ${\mathrm{Bi}_{2}S e_{3}}/{\mathrm{NC}}/{\mathrm{S}}.$ the two reduction peaks move positively coupled with the two oxidation peaks  moving  negatively,  indicating  that $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ offers  less overpotential  for  sulfur  species  transformation. Figure  3(b) demonstrates the charge–discharge curves of the typical number of  cycles  of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ at $0.1~\mathrm{C}.$ The  two  discharge  voltage platforms  correspond  to  the  conversion  of  sulfur  to  LiPSs,  and then to $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S},$ while the charge voltage platform corresponds to the reverse process. The reaction platforms remain in good condition after  100  cycles,  indicating  favorable  stability.  The  galvanostatic cycling curves at $0.1\mathrm{~C~}$ are presented in Fig. 3(c). By comparison, the  maintained  capacity  of  the $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ electrode  is $205\mathrm{\mAh{g}^{-1}}$ higher  than  that  of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ after  100  cycles, which  demonstrates  the  satisfactory  sulfur-fixation  capacity  and easy  redox  kinetics  brought  about  by  defect  engineering. Furthermore, the two electrodes are subjected to a long-term cycle performance test at $3\mathrm{C}$ for 1,100 cycles to further demonstrate the effect  of  defect  engineering  on  cycle  stability.  As  displayed  in Fig. 3(e),  the $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ electrode  exhibits  more  admirable stability  and  a  fairly  lower  capacity  decay  rate  of  approximately $0.0278\%$ per  cycle  than $0.06\%$ of ${\tt B i}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3}/{\tt N C}/{\tt S}$ .  In  addition, Fig. 3(d) demonstrates  the  superior  rate  performance  of  the $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ electrode  which  exhibits $435\mathrm{\mAh{g}^{-1}}$ at $_5\mathrm{~C~}$ and remains at $847\mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ when return to $0.2\mathrm{C},$ further manifesting the remarkable stability and highly reversibility of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ . In addition, the cycling performance based on $a{\mathrm{-Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm S}{\mathrm e}_{3-x}/{\mathrm N}{\mathrm C}/{\mathrm S}$ at different  defect  levels  is  compared  (Fig. S6  in  the  ESM),  among which $60{-}\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3{-}x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ achieves the highest capacity and optimal cycling  performance  caused  by  its  modest  selenium  vacancies level, outstanding electrical conductivity, and complete internal structure.
The  realization  of  high  energy  density  Li-S  batteries  calls  for increasing  cathode  sulfur  loading  and  decreasing  electrolyte consumption. Given this, the cyclic performance of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ with increasing sulfur loadings of 1.3, 2.9, and $6.2\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ is tested over  100  cycles  at $0.2~\mathrm{C}.$ .  Thanks  to  the  strong  adsorption  and superior  charge  transfer  properties  of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC},$ their  initial discharge capacity, retentive capacity, and capacity decay rate after 100 cycles of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ with a $1.3~\mathrm{mg{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ sulfur content are $1,065.86\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ , $505.85\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ ,  and $0.5\%$ ; $2.9\mathrm{\mg{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ were $1,118.52\mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ , $644.18\mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ ,  and $0.4\%$ ; $6.2\mathrm{\mg{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ were $1,169.12\ \mathrm{mAh\cdotg^{-1}}$ , $808.35\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ , and $0.3\%$ (Fig. S7 in the ESM). The  rate  performance  of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ is  also  examined  with  a $6.2~\mathrm{\mg{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ sulfur  content  (Fig. 3(f)).  The $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ still demonstrates  a  discharge  capacity  of $455\ \mathrm{mAh{g}^{-1}}$ at  a $^\textrm{\scriptsize1C}$ rate coupled with a little change of discharge capacity when the rate returns to $0.2\mathrm{~C~}$ compared with the initial $0.2~\mathrm{C},$ suggesting that the  electrode  still  offers  excellent  stability  and  reversibility  even under  high  sulfur  loading.  Moreover,  as  shown  in Fig. $3(\mathrm{g)}$ ,  the typical  two  platforms  are  still  visible  in  the  galvanostatic discharge–charge curves of the $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ with a $6.2~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ sulfur  content  benefiting  from  the  unique  selenium  vacancies design in ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3-x}/{\mathrm{NC}}.$ These results further confirm the significant advantages  of  the $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ design  in  the  pursuit  of  highperformance  Li-S  batteries.  As  a  demonstration  of  practical application,  the  battery  with $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ is  capable  of  powering light-emitting  diodes  (Fig. 3(h)),  indicating  good  energy  storage capability and bright application prospects.
To  understand  the  potential  mechanism  of  electrode electrochemical improvement of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC},$ we employ density functional theory (DFT) to clarify its optimized electron states and excellent charge transport properties. The top-view and side-view structures of ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}$ are shown in Fig. S9 in the ESM. Figure S10 in the ESM depicts the computational simulations of the  interactions  between $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}$ (or ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3})$ and  sulfur  species regarding the full sulfurization state during battery charging and discharging. The adsorption energies of each sulfur species on the (101) lattice plane of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}$ and ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3}$ are calculated as shown in Fig. 4(a).  Specifically,  the  adsorption  energies  of ${\sf S}_{8},$ $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8},$ $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6},$ $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4},$ $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ on $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}$ surface are $+0.18.$ , $-0.16$ , $-0.60,$ −1.70, $-1.73$ ,  and $-2.73\mathrm{~}\mathrm{eV}$ ,  respectively,  which  all  higher  than those on ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3}$ surface are $+0.57$ , $-0.12$ , $-0.11$ , $-0.23$ , $-0.83$ , and $-1.53\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. This result confirms the tremendous ability of  defect  engineering  to  enhance  LiPSs  adsorption  for  sulfur electrochemistry. The transformation of soluble $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ to insoluble $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm S_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm S$ is a key step, thereby $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ is a significant intermediate to illuminate  the  electrochemical  interaction  of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3}/\mathrm{NC}$ or $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ on LiPSs [32]. As shown in Fig. 4(b), according to the differences  of  electron  density  between  the  two  samples  for  the adsorption  structure  of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4},$ the  surface  electrostatic  potential plots  were  employed  to  reveal  the  surface  chemical  interaction. Based  on  the  calculation  results,  the  interaction  between  the  S atom  and  Bi  atom  dominated  the  main  adsorption  behavior  of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4},$ and a powerful Bi–S bond is formed due to the electrons of the S atoms prefer to flow toward the Bi atoms. In fact, the charge transfer amount between Bi and S on the $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}$ surface is more than  that  on ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3}$ .  The  surface  charge  density  and  surface differential charge density are calculated to indicate more electrons flowed in/out of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}$ surface and charge accumulation around selenium  vacancies,  which  indicates  that  selenium  vacancies promote the electron contacts between $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}$ and sulfur species, thus enhancing the polar adsorption of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ (Fig. $4(\mathrm{c}))$ . In addition,  the  kinetic  process  of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ decomposition  has  been studied  based  on  the  climbing  image  nudged  elastic  band  (CINEB) method, which is given in Fig. 4(d). The $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ decomposition has  a  much  lower  energy  barrier  of $0.02\ \mathrm{eV}$ on  the  selenium vacancies-rich $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}$ surface  than  that  on  pristine ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3}$ (0.54 $\mathrm{eV},$ ), indicating the facilitated $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ transformation kinetics thanks to the superiority of selenium vacancies. Together, the DFT results show  that  the  defective $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}$ holds  a  broad  prospect  as  an efficient sulfur electrocatalyst.
Selenium defect and optimized electronic structure enable the as-designed $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ to  exhibit  strong  sulfur  constraints  and fast redox kinetics (Fig. 5(a)). The superior LiPSs adsorption of the $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ can  be  further  visualized  by  immersing  the  same amount of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ and ${\mathrm{Bi}_{2}S e_{3}}/{\mathrm{NC}}$ in the 1,2- dimethoxyethane:1,3-dioxolane (DME:DOL, 1:1) solution of 0.05 M $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ and  standing  for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ to  observe  the  superior  LiPSs adsorption  performance  of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ (Fig. S8  in  the  ESM). Apparently, the color of the $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC-Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ solution gradually changed  from  brown  to  nearly  transparent  after $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ ,  while  the color of ${\mathrm{Bi}_{2}S e_{3}/N C}{\cdot}{\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}}$ solution still remained distinctly brown, exhibiting a strong visual absorption effect realized by $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ (Fig. 5(b)). In addition, symmetrical cells based on $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3}/\mathrm{NC}$ and $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ serve as a proof-of-concept to compare and evaluate the catalytic ability of the as-designed $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ [33]. Based on the  multi-step  conversion  reaction  mechanism  of  sulfur electrochemistry,  CV  curves  of  symmetric  batteries  at  different scanning rates of $0.1{-}0.5\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{S}^{-1}$ were studied, which are exhibited in Figs.  5(c) and 5(d).  With  the  increase  of  scanning  rate,  the anodic peaks in the cathode had a significant positive move, while the  cathodic  peaks  had  an  opposite  move,  resulting  in  an increasing  polarization  voltage  at  higher  scanning  rates.  By contrast, the two redox peaks of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ moved slightly with the  increasing  scanning  rates,  indicating  the  fast  and  facilitated sulfur  redox  reaction.  To  further  investigate  the  superiority  of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ to sulfur species conversion kinetics, we investigated the  corresponding  lithium-ion  diffusion  properties.  The  Li-ion diffusion  coefficient  can  be  estimated  from  the  classical Randles–Sevcik equation
$$
I_{\mathrm{p}}=(2.69\times10^{5})n^{0.5}A D^{0.5}C\nu^{0.5}
$$
where $I_{\mathrm{p}}$ is the peak current, $n$ is the number of reacting electrons, $A$ is  the  electrode  area, $D$ is  the  diffusion  coefficient  of  lithium ions, $C$ is the concentration of lithium ions in the electrolyte, and $\nu$ is the scanning rate. Given this, all reduction and oxidation peaks are linear with the square root of the scanning rate [34–36]. The slope  of  the $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ electrode  is  higher  than  that  of  the $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ electrode  in  each  sulfur  redox  reaction,  indicating faster  Li-ion  diffusion  rate  and  improved  sulfur  redox  kinetics during  cycling  (Figs.  5(e) and 5(f)).  Electrochemical  impedance spectroscopy  (EIS)  spectra  revealed  that  the $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ symmetric cell has a smaller electrochemical resistance, which is consistent  with  the  density  of  states  calculated  above  (Fig. $5(\mathrm{g})_{.}^{.}$ ). This  is  prone  to  sulfur  redox  reactions. $75\%$ of  the  discharge capacity  of  Li-S  batteries  is  determined  by  the  solid–liquid conversion  from $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ to $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ .  Therefore,  the  nucleationdeposition of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ on the electrode plays an important role in the overall  sulfur  reaction.  To  further  investigate  the  outstanding catalytic effect of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC},$ the so-called nucleation-deposition experiment was carried out based on $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ electrodes and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}/$ /tetraglyme  electrolyte,  and  the  potentiostatic  discharge profiles  of  different  cells  are  shown  in  the Fig. 5(h) [13, 37, 38]. The  value  of  the  line  integral  of  current  indicates  the  ability  of nucleation  deposition  of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ .  Thereby,  the  capacity  of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ deposition on $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ surface is distinctly higher than that of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC},$ which further proves that $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ has a distinctly catalytic effect on the sulfur reaction. Altogether, the above results demonstrate the excellent anchoring ability and catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ owing  to  the  structure  construction  and  defect engineering toward expedited and stable Li-S electrochemistry.
## 3 Conclusions
In  conclusion,  the  narrow-bandgap $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ with  different selenium  vacancies  amounts  was  developed  via  a  selenization pyrolysis followed by a thermal shock process. The experimental results and DFT investigation infer that selenium deficiencies give $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}$ improved  electronic  conductivity,  enhanced  sulfur affinity, and optimized catalytic activity, which not only inhibits shuttle behavior of LiPSs, but also promotes sulfur redox kinetics. Combined  with  the  highly-conductive  porous  N  doped  carbon derived  from  the  pyrolysis  of  Bi-MOF  precursor, $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{Se}_{3-x}/\mathrm{NC}/\mathrm{S}$ cathode  contributes  to  a  fairly  lower  capacity  decay  rate  of approximately $0.0278\%$ per cycle over 1,000 cycles at $3\mathrm{C}$ . Even at the  high  sulfur  loading  of $6.2~\mathrm{\mg{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ ,  the  cathode  still demonstrates  a  discharge  capacity  of $455\mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg^{-1}}}$ at $^\textrm{\scriptsize1C}$ .  Our work completes the enhanced mechanism of Se vacancy and may facilitate  the  development  of  anionic  vacancy  as  well  as  highperformance Li-S batteries.
